    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elam">Elam</a> joined lowland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#susa">Susa</a> and highland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anshan">Anshan</a> in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>, a pairing that let it survive conquest and revive for two millennia.</li><li>It repeatedly broke Mesopotamian powers, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ur">Ur</a> and toppling Kassite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a>, and its rulers once outranked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#susa">Susa</a> c. 646 BCE, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elamite-language">Elamite language</a> and bureaucracy lived on inside the Persian Achaemenid state.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#susa">Susa</a>, not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a>: Elamite kings carried it off as a war trophy c. 1158 BCE, and French excavators recovered it around 1900.</p></aside>
    </section>

